inal signed order.\n\n### Workshop station 1\nThe mirror search UI clipped long tokens at a fixed display width. Its snippet could locate a package, but did not guarantee that two packages with the same visible prefix matched.\n\nThe station file was retained as a reference exercise. Its facilitator could recommend clearer filing, but had no authority to alter Tidal Bundle's signed policy or make a binding decision for the requested case.\n\n### Workshop station 2\nThe digest tokens in this scenario are literal fictional strings. No hidden hexadecimal decoding, case folding, or semantic completion rule is part of their comparison contract.\n\nThe station file was retained as a reference exercise. Its facilitator could recommend clearer filing, but had no authority to alter Tidal Bundle's signed policy or make a binding decision for the requested case.\n\n### Workshop station 3\nThe steward split a full token into a base and a separately signed suffix. A reader had to preserve the leading punctuation when reconstructing the exact accepted token.\n\nThe station file was retained as a reference exercise. Its facilitator could recommend clearer filing, but had no authority to alter Tidal Bundle's signed policy or make a binding decision for the requested case.\n\n### Workshop station 4\nPractice excerpts sometimes displayed a typographic ellipsis. The ellipsis signaled omitted characters and was not itself a token character or proof of a unique completion.\n\nThe station file was retained as a reference exercise. Its facilitator could recommend clearer filing, but had no authority to alter Tidal Bundle's signed policy or make a binding decision for the requested case.\n\n### Workshop station 5\nThe recovery package and ordinary package had related names. Their authoritative manifests were separate, so an ordinary suffix could not be borrowed to finish a recovery prefix.\n\nThe station file was retained as a reference exercise. Its facilitator could recommend clearer fil